python binding for yupiter
==========================

A PyO3 binding of the public C17 interface of yupiter to Python.
The binding is not part of the library build; it lives in python/
and is built with maturin.  It links against the installed shared
object (libyupiter.so) and exposes output and allocation functions from
include/yupiter.h.

Requirements
------------

  * Rust 1.83 or newer.
  * Python 3.12 or newer.
  * A built yupiter library ("make" in the repository root).
  * patchelf, for the wheel packaging step.

Build
-----

    cd python
    maturin build --release

The wheel is written to python/target/wheels/.  Install it with:

    pip install python/target/wheels/yupiter-*.whl

For iterative development:

    maturin develop --release

Structure
---------

    python/Cargo.toml         Rust crate manifest; abi3-py312.
    python/pyproject.toml     maturin build system configuration.
    python/build.rs           Builds yupiter and links libyupiter.so.
    python/src/lib.rs         The binding: FFI declarations and the
                              #[pymodule] definition.
    python/yupiter_py/        Python package init.

Exposed symbols
---------------

Output:
    yupiter_printf(format) -> int | None
    yupiter_fprintf(stream, format) -> int | None

Memory:
    yupiter_malloc(size) -> int | None
    yupiter_free(ptr)
    yupiter_realloc(ptr, size) -> int | None

Safe memory:
    yupiter_malloc_safe(count, size) -> int
    yupiter_free_safe(ptr)
    yupiter_realloc_safe(ptr, count, size) -> int

Constants:
    yupiter.STDOUT, yupiter.STDERR, yupiter.STDIN

Design notes
------------

The binding links against libyupiter.so (dylib), not libyupiter.a.
The static archive contains the freestanding startup file, which
defines _start and conflicts with the Python interpreter's entry
point.  The shared object excludes it.

The FFI declarations use link_name to reference the C symbols
(yupiter_printf, etc.) while the Rust functions are named the same.
This avoids the duplicate-name error that would otherwise arise.

The Python safe allocator variants use non-terminating native operations.
They raise OverflowError for size overflow, MemoryError for exhaustion,
and ValueError for invalid, freed or corrupt pointers.  Zero-size
allocation returns 0; zero-size resize releases the block and returns 0.
New allocations are zero-filled; growth preserves existing bytes and
leaves additional bytes uninitialised.  Native C safe functions keep
terminating with status 134 on failure.

This repairs the documented promise of Python exceptions.  Earlier
bindings terminated the interpreter on native failures and raised a
PyO3 PanicException on multiplication overflow; callers should now catch
the standard Python exceptions above.

printf and fprintf accept a pre-formatted string.  The variadic C
function cannot be bound directly by the PyO3 macro, so formatting
is done in Python before the call.  Both wrappers pass a constant
"%s" native format, so percent directives in the text print literally.

Pointer arguments and return values are passed as integers.  This
matches the freestanding design, which does not expose the layout
of yupiter_FILE.

